WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: zh-TW

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.966
In 2013,

00:00:00.966 --> 00:00:03.500
a striking scene unfolded in Thailand.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:04.333
A political party

00:00:04.333 --> 00:00:06.066
won the most votes nationwide

00:00:06.066 --> 00:00:07.500
and became the largest party in parliament

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:08.566
—yet its leader 

00:00:08.566 --> 00:00:09.966
was unable to become prime minister

00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:11.333
The party's leader is Pita Limcharoenrat.

00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:11.933
That year,

00:00:11.933 --> 00:00:13.700
he was just one step away from the premiership.

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.000
What stopped him was not public opinion, nor the ballot box, 

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.866
but an unreasonable system.

00:00:17.866 --> 00:00:20.700
I’m Hsiang-Yuan, a Taiwanese editor based in the United States,

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.900
currently a senior editor for Southeast Asia at The News Lens.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:25.133
Today, we are not talking about

00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.100
the rise of a political star.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.066
We are talking about a system

00:00:28.066 --> 00:00:31.066
—and how it shut the door on democracy with its own hands.

00:00:31.066 --> 00:00:32.566
Before we get into the main video,

00:00:32.574 --> 00:00:34.500
don’t forget to 

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.533
like our channel, subscribe,

00:00:35.533 --> 00:00:37.033
and turn on the notification bell! 

00:00:37.054 --> 00:00:39.502
How did Pita rise in Thai politics?

00:00:40.066 --> 00:00:42.933
Pita is not a typical Thai politician.

00:00:42.933 --> 00:00:44.400
He comes from an elite background,

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.633
studied at Harvard and MIT,

00:00:46.633 --> 00:00:47.866
plays the guitar,

00:00:47.866 --> 00:00:50.200
and counts Seattle’s band Nirvana as his idol.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.066
He originally worked in the private company,

00:00:52.066 --> 00:00:55.166
living a life that required no involvement in politics at all.

00:00:55.172 --> 00:00:58.233
Everything changed after he listened to a speech by Thanathorn,

00:00:58.236 --> 00:00:59.566
the leader of the Future Forward Party. Inspired, Pita decided to enter politics.

00:00:59.568 --> 00:01:02.566
At Thanathorn’s invitation, he joined the party list and,

00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:06.666
in 2019, was elected to Thailand’s House of Representatives as the fourth-ranked party-list MP.

00:01:06.679 --> 00:01:08.100
Let’s pause here for a moment,

00:01:08.112 --> 00:01:09.900
because if you’re not familiar with Thanathorn,

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.866
it’s hard to understand just how difficult the road ahead was for Pita.

00:01:12.874 --> 00:01:15.800
Thanathorn rose rapidly in Thai politics in 2018.

00:01:15.805 --> 00:01:17.500
Young, idealistic, 

00:01:17.504 --> 00:01:19.066
and charismatic,

00:01:19.066 --> 00:01:22.100
he quickly became a political star among the younger generation.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:24.200
In the March 2019 general election,

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:26.366
he led the then one-year-old Future Forward Party 

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.679
to become Thailand’s third-largest party.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.066
But the momentum did not last.

00:01:29.066 --> 00:01:30.366
In February 2020,

00:01:30.366 --> 00:01:32.800
the Constitutional Court dissolved the Future Forward Party.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.333
Sixteen party members, including Thanathorn, 

00:01:35.339 --> 00:01:37.005
were banned from politics for ten years.

00:01:37.066 --> 00:01:38.400
For Pita,

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:39.700
this meant that in his very first year in politics,

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.666
he lost the mentor who had brought him into the political arena.

00:01:42.666 --> 00:01:43.533
Overnight,

00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:45.066
he went from a freshman legislator

00:01:45.066 --> 00:01:47.233
to a party leader thrust onto the front line.

00:01:47.263 --> 00:01:49.844
Not a “political golden boy,” but a “designated survivor”

00:01:50.266 --> 00:01:52.333
Many people first truly noticed Pita

00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:55.866
during the height of his popularity before the May 2023 election.

00:01:55.866 --> 00:01:57.333
At campaign rallies,

00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:58.366
his baby face was

00:01:58.366 --> 00:02:01.600
often framed by yellow flower garlands given by supporters.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.033
The nickname “political golden boy”

00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:05.633
quickly spread across media and social platforms

00:02:05.633 --> 00:02:07.533
But Pita himself saw it differently.

00:02:07.533 --> 00:02:10.766
He compared his situation to the 

00:02:10.766 --> 00:02:12.633
character Tom in the TV series Designated Survivor

00:02:12.633 --> 00:02:15.033
—not because he was the most qualified to lead,

00:02:15.033 --> 00:02:17.200
but because everyone else had already been cleared out.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:19.866
In his political memoir The Almost Prime Minister

00:02:19.866 --> 00:02:21.400
Pita wrote bluntly:

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:23.000
“Before the dissolution of Future Forward,

00:02:23.084 --> 00:02:26.514
Thanathorn’s approval rating was 31 percent, the highest in the country.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:27.700
After he took over,

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.233
support fell all the way to 3 percent

00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:32.833
—the last among six party leaders.”

00:02:32.833 --> 00:02:34.200
But he refused to take shortcuts.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.766
Instead, he chose the slowest and most grounded path

00:02:36.766 --> 00:02:39.500
—traveling to rural provinces, entering villages, and listening to farmers.

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:42.801
“Families who have lived off farming for seven generations still can’t obtain land titles.”

00:02:42.834 --> 00:02:47.264
“To legally brew rice wine, you need ten million baht in registered capital.”

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:48.833
He brought these obvious yet long

00:02:48.833 --> 00:02:51.933
-ignored livelihood issues into parliament.

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:53.433
He also relied heavily on data analysis

00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:56.033
to make decisions that maximized his team’s effectiveness.

00:02:56.033 --> 00:02:57.266
In his book, he asked:

00:02:57.266 --> 00:03:01.053
“When did MPs sleeping in parliament become normal?"

00:03:01.119 --> 00:03:03.001
"When did vote-buying become normal?"

00:03:03.051 --> 00:03:07.014
"When did parliament failing to reach quorum 36 times in four years become normal?”

00:03:07.514 --> 00:03:13.343
“And when drafting policy bills, how did it become unremarkable to invite capitalists who dominate the industry to take part?”

00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:15.000
By calling out these realities

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.700
that everyone had grown used to,

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.866
Pita’s support began to rebound 

00:03:18.866 --> 00:03:20.866
after nearly a year as party leader

00:03:21.003 --> 00:03:23.501
By March 2023, his approval ranking had risen to second place.

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:24.866
One month before the election,

00:03:24.866 --> 00:03:28.996
roughly 5.7 million social media posts mentioned his name

00:03:29.013 --> 00:03:31.344
Still unable to escape dissolution

00:03:31.966 --> 00:03:35.233
After the Future Forward Party was dissolved,

00:03:35.233 --> 00:03:37.400
a reporter asked Pita

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:38.800
at the press conference for the new Move Forward Party,

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.433
“Do you think this party will also be dissolved?”

00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:42.700
Pita froze.

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:44.966
He later said that only in a system where legal mechanisms

00:03:44.966 --> 00:03:47.266
are routinely weaponized against political parties

00:03:47.266 --> 00:03:51.600
would such an absurd question be asked before a party

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.900
had even been given a chance to govern.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:54.833
Unfortunately,

00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:57.433
as Move Forward’s popularity surged

00:03:57.433 --> 00:04:00.600
—and after it won the most votes nationwide in May 2023

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:01.900
—the same script 

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.333
that had been used against Thanathorn 

00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:06.233
was replayed against Pita.

00:04:06.233 --> 00:04:08.400
First came the prime ministerial vote.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.100
Thailand’s parliament consists of both the House and the Senate,

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.500
and senators aligned with the military either abstained

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:17.200
or were absent when voting on Pita, 

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:19.433
leaving the prime ministership undecided.

00:04:19.433 --> 00:04:20.000
Next,

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.233
the Constitutional Court suspended Pita from his position as an MP 

00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:25.300
on the grounds that he held shares in a media company.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.666
儘管後來又復職Although he was later reinstated,

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:29.300
the political landscape had already shifted.

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:31.366
The turmoil did not end there.

00:04:31.366 --> 00:04:32.700
In August 2024,

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.433
the Constitutional Court dissolved the Move Forward Party,

00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:39.266
citing its advocacy to amend Section 112 of the Criminal Code—the lèse-majesté law. 

00:04:39.266 --> 00:04:41.133
Multiple party members, including Pita, 

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:43.166
were banned from politics for ten years.

00:04:43.166 --> 00:04:45.766
In a later speech at Harvard,

00:04:45.766 --> 00:04:49.066
Pita reflected that Move Forward had won on three core principles:

00:04:49.066 --> 00:04:50.733
the military’s withdrawal from politics, 

00:04:50.733 --> 00:04:53.466
the dismantling of monopolies, and the weakening of excessive centralization.

00:04:53.466 --> 00:04:53.966
Yet it was precisely 

00:04:53.966 --> 00:04:55.900
these three principles 

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.066
that prevented him from becoming prime minister.

00:04:58.066 --> 00:05:00.700
The “designated survivor” who endured to the very end

00:05:00.705 --> 00:05:02.870
 ultimately lost to the system itself. 

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:04.033
Once again, 

00:05:04.033 --> 00:05:05.833
the door to democracy was slammed shut.

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:08.833
Thailand will hold another general election on February 8.

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:11.100
Whether the People’s Party—seen as the successor to Move Forward

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.466
—can break through and become the largest party in parliament,

00:05:13.466 --> 00:05:14.300
and whether public opinion 

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:16.700
can successfully challenge the existing system, 

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:18.366
will be key questions to watch.

00:05:18.366 --> 00:05:20.366
The video also shares that 

00:05:20.366 --> 00:05:22.400
after casting his vote on February 8,

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.066
Pita will attend the Taipei International Book Exhibition 

00:05:24.066 --> 00:05:25.633
to promote the Chinese edition of his memoir 

00:05:25.633 --> 00:05:27.600
The Unfinished Road.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:28.633
It will be a rare opportunity 

00:05:28.633 --> 00:05:30.333
in Taiwan to engage closely with the realities 

00:05:30.333 --> 00:05:32.666
of Thai democracy.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:37.100
That concludes this episode of APA KHABAR

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:39.833
If you have thoughts on Pita’s political journey, 

00:05:39.833 --> 00:05:41.500
or if you’ve read The Almost Prime Minister,

00:05:41.500 --> 00:05:44.566
feel free to leave a comment below and share your views.

00:05:44.566 --> 00:05:45.366
I’m Hsiang-Yuan. 

00:05:45.366 --> 00:05:46.433
Thank you for watching, 

00:05:46.433 --> 00:05:47.833
and see you next time.

00:05:47.833 --> 00:05:49.833
And finally, don’t forget to 

00:05:49.833 --> 00:05:50.833
like, subscribe, 

